export const memorials=[{id:"india-gate",name:"India Gate",image:"assets/memorials/india-gate.jpg",city:"New Delhi",state:"Delhi",coordinates:{lat:28.6129,lng:77.2295},built:"1931",architect:"Sir Edwin Lutyens",significance:"India Gate is a war memorial dedicated to the 70,000 Indian soldiers who died fighting for the British Indian Army during World War I and the Third Anglo-Afghan War. The names of over 13,300 soldiers are inscribed on its surface.",history:"Designed by Sir Edwin Lutyens, India Gate was built between 1921 and 1931 as a memorial to Indian soldiers who served in World War I. The foundation stone was laid in 1921 by the Duke of Connaught. Originally known as the All India War Memorial, the 42-metre-high arch stands at the eastern end of the Rajpath (now Kartavya Path). After the Bangladesh Liberation War of 1971, an eternal flame (Amar Jawan Jyoti) was added beneath the arch. In 2022, the National War Memorial was built nearby to honor post-independence sacrifices, while India Gate continues to serve as a symbol of national pride and sacrifice.",keyFacts:["42 meters tall — one of the largest war memorials in India","Names of 13,300 soldiers inscribed on the surface","Built between 1921 and 1931 by Sir Edwin Lutyens","Amar Jawan Jyoti (eternal flame) was added in 1972","Part of the Rajpath (Kartavya Path) ceremonial axis","Over 1 million visitors annually"],architecturalStyle:"Triumphal arch",visitingHours:"Open 24 hours (illuminated at night)",status:"Active monument under ASI",color:"#FF6B35"},{id:"national-war-memorial",name:"National War Memorial",image:"assets/memorials/national-war-memorial.jpg",city:"New Delhi",state:"Delhi",coordinates:{lat:28.6119,lng:77.2285},built:"2019",architect:"CP Kukreja Architects",significance:"The National War Memorial honors the 26,466 Indian soldiers who have sacrificed their lives in post-independence conflicts including the Indo-China War (1962), Indo-Pak Wars (1947, 1965, 1971, 1999), UN Peacekeeping missions, and the Sri Lanka intervention.",history:"For decades after independence, India lacked a dedicated war memorial for its post-1947 military sacrifices. The idea was conceived in 2006 and the foundation stone was laid by Prime Minister Narendra Modi on February 25, 2015. The memorial was inaugurated on February 25, 2019, spanning 40 acres near India Gate. It features four concentric circles — the Amar Chakra (Circle of Immortality), Veer Chakra (Circle of Bravery), Tyag Chakra (Circle of Sacrifice), and Rakshak Chakra (Circle of Protection). The central obelisk is flanked by bronze murals depicting India's military campaigns.",keyFacts:["Spans 40 acres near India Gate","Honors 26,466 post-independence military sacrifices","Four concentric circles representing different aspects of valor","Inaugurated on February 25, 2019","Over 1,500 bronze murals depicting military campaigns","24x7 eternal flame at the center"],architecturalStyle:"Modern Indian with concentric circle design",visitingHours:"9:00 AM – 7:30 PM (Monday to Sunday)",status:"Active memorial under Ministry of Defence",color:"#1E3A5F"},{id:"raj-ghat",name:"Raj Ghat",image:"assets/memorials/raj-ghat.jpg",city:"New Delhi",state:"Delhi",coordinates:{lat:28.641,lng:77.247},built:"1948",architect:"Raghubir Singh (original); later redesigned by various architects",significance:"Raj Ghat is the memorial dedicated to Mahatma Gandhi, the Father of the Nation. It marks the spot where Mahatma Gandhi was cremated on January 31, 1948, the day after his assassination. It serves as a place of pilgrimage for those who believe in non-violence and peace.",history:"After Mahatma Gandhi's assassination on January 30, 1948, his body was brought to Raj Ghat for cremation the following day. The memorial was designed as a simple black marble platform that marks the spot of the cremation. The word 'Hey Ram' — Gandhi's last words — are inscribed on the platform. The surrounding garden, originally designed by Raghubir Singh, features a peaceful landscape with trees planted by visiting dignitaries including Queen Elizabeth II, Ho Chi Minh, and Nelson Mandela. The memorial was officially inaugurated on August 15, 1948. Every Friday, a prayer meeting is held at the memorial.",keyFacts:["Marks the cremation site of Mahatma Gandhi (Jan 31, 1948)","'Hey Ram' — Gandhi's last words — inscribed on the platform","Trees planted by dignitaries including Queen Elizabeth II and Nelson Mandela","Friday prayer meetings held every week","One of the most visited memorials in India","Surrounded by a serene garden with an eternal flame"],architecturalStyle:"Minimalist — black marble platform in a garden setting",visitingHours:"6:30 AM – 6:00 PM (Closed on Fridays for public)",status:"Active memorial under Gandhi Smriti",color:"#2D5A27"},{id:"cellular-jail",name:"Cellular Jail",image:"assets/memorials/cellular-jail.jpg",city:"Port Blair",state:"Andaman and Nicobar Islands",coordinates:{lat:11.6234,lng:92.726},built:"1906",architect:"British colonial administration (designed by surveillance experts)",significance:"The Cellular Jail, also known as 'Kala Pani' (Black Waters), was a colonial prison used by the British to exile political prisoners during the Indian freedom struggle. It witnessed the sacrifice of many freedom fighters and is a powerful symbol of India's struggle for independence.",history:"Built between 1896 and 1906, the Cellular Jail was designed to hold political prisoners in solitary confinement — hence the name 'Cellular' as each cell was designed for one prisoner. The prison had seven wings radiating from a central tower, creating a panopticon structure where a single guard could watch all wings. Notable prisoners included Veer Savarkar, Batukeshwar Dutt, and many others. During World War II, the Japanese occupied the Andaman Islands and used the prison for their own purposes. After independence, the jail was declared a national memorial in 1969. Today, three of the original seven wings remain, along with a museum and a light-and-sound show that narrates the history of the freedom struggle.",keyFacts:["7 wings with 698 cells — each cell designed for solitary confinement","Known as 'Kala Pani' (Black Waters) by prisoners","Built between 1896 and 1906","Declared a national memorial on February 11, 1979","Only 3 of the original 7 wings survive today","Light-and-sound show narrates the freedom struggle"],architecturalStyle:"Panopticon — radiating wings from a central tower",visitingHours:"9:00 AM – 5:00 PM (Closed on Mondays)",status:"National Memorial under ASI",color:"#8B4513"},{id:"jallianwala-bagh",name:"Jallianwala Bagh",image:"assets/memorials/jallianwala-bagh.jpg",city:"Amritsar",state:"Punjab",coordinates:{lat:31.6209,lng:74.881},built:"1951",architect:"Government of India (memorial); original site dates to pre-1919",significance:"Jallianwala Bagh is the site of the horrific massacre that took place on April 13, 1919, when British troops under General Dyer fired on a peaceful gathering of thousands, killing hundreds. It stands as a stark reminder of colonial brutality and the sacrifices made during the freedom struggle.",history:"On April 13, 1919 — the day of Baisakhi — a peaceful gathering of thousands assembled at Jallianwala Bagh to protest the Rowlatt Act and demand the release of Dr. Saifuddin Kitchlew and Dr. Satya Pal. General Reginald Dyer ordered his troops to fire on the unarmed crowd without warning. The bullets continued for about 10 minutes, and the narrow exits made escape nearly impossible. Official British estimates placed the death toll at 379, while Indian estimates suggest over 1,000 were killed and 1,500 wounded. The bullet marks on the walls and the well into which people jumped to save their lives are preserved as evidence of the massacre. The site was converted into a memorial in 1951. The 'Flame of Liberty' memorial was unveiled in 2001 by President APJ Abdul Kalam.",keyFacts:["April 13, 1919 — the Baisakhi massacre","Official death toll: 379; Indian estimates: over 1,,000","Bullet marks preserved on the walls of the enclosed garden","The 'Martyrs' Well' — people jumped in to escape bullets","Memorial established in 1951 by the Indian government","'Flame of Liberty' unveiled in 2001 by President Kalam"],architecturalStyle:"Garden memorial with preserved historical walls",visitingHours:"6:30 AM – 7:30 PM (Open all days)",status:"National Memorial under Jallianwala Bagh Memorial Trust",color:"#B22222"},{id:"sabarmati-ashram",name:"Sabarmati Ashram",image:"assets/memorials/sabarmati-ashram.jpg",city:"Ahmedabad",state:"Gujarat",coordinates:{lat:23.061,lng:72.5795},built:"1917",architect:"Charles Correa (museum); Mahatma Gandhi (ashram layout)",significance:"Sabarmati Ashram, also known as Gandhi Ashram, was the residence of Mahatma Gandhi from 1917 to 1930. It served as the nerve center of India's freedom movement and was the starting point of the historic Dandi March (Salt Satyagraha). The ashram embodies Gandhi's principles of simplicity, self-reliance, and non-violence.",history:"Founded by Mahatma Gandhi on June 17, 1917, on the banks of the Sabarmati River, the ashram was relocated from Kochrab Ashram due to its more suitable location. Gandhi chose this site because it was between a jail and a crematorium — symbolizing the choice between prison and death that freedom fighters faced. The ashram housed over 70 inmates and served as a community living space where spinning, weaving, and basic education were practiced. From here, Gandhi launched several important movements including the Dandi March on March 12, 1930. The ashram was vacated in 1930 as per Gandhi's promise to the British that he would return only after independence. Today, the Gandhi Smriti and Darshan Trust manages the ashram, which houses a museum (Gandhi Smarak Sangrahalaya) with over 34,000 artifacts including photographs, manuscripts, and personal belongings of Gandhi.",keyFacts:["Mahatma Gandhi's residence from 1917 to 1930","Starting point of the Dandi March (March 12, 1930)","Houses over 34,000 artifacts in the Gandhi Smarak Sangrahalaya","Spans 36 acres along the Sabarmati River","Over 1 million visitors annually","Designated as a Grade I heritage building"],architecturalStyle:"Simple Gujarati haveli-style with modern museum",visitingHours:"8:30 AM – 6:30 PM (Open all days)",status:"Heritage site under Gandhi Smriti and Darshan Trust",color:"#DAA520"}];export const memorialTabs=[{id:"overview",label:"Overview"},{id:"history",label:"History"},{id:"locations",label:"Locations"},{id:"facts",label:"Facts"}];